1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,600
You've just taken over a teammate's Python project.

2
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:06,700
The deadline is tomorrow.

3
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:10,500
You run the tests, and this is what you get.

4
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:12,250
Which error matters?

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:13,850
Where do you start?

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,750
"No module named helper" looks like something to install.

7
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:22,156
But helper is a file in your own project,

8
00:00:22,196 --> 00:00:26,700
and installing a package with that name would not help.

9
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:31,700
Mapping was removed from collections in Python 3.10.

10
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:35,100
numpy.float is gone from newer NumPy.

11
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:41,600
Every error needs a different kind of fix, and fixing one can uncover the next.

12
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:44,700
AI assistants help, but not always.

13
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:51,221
In the 2025 Stack Overflow survey, two thirds of developers who named a frustration

14
00:00:51,261 --> 00:00:56,850
with AI tools pointed to answers that are almost right, but not quite.

15
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:02,400
And after you change something, how do you know it is really fixed?

16
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:06,500
FixFirst sorts out the mess.

17
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:11,500
It finds what to fix first, and tells you when it is really fixed.

18
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:15,914
It runs your project's own tests with your project's own Python,

19
00:01:15,954 --> 00:01:21,050
explains each failure with evidence, and gives you one concrete next step.

20
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:24,600
It never edits your code or installs anything by itself.

21
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:26,450
You stay in control.

22
00:01:27,805 --> 00:01:30,912
Let's try it on a small shop project. I choose the folder,

23
00:01:30,952 --> 00:01:33,255
FixFirst finds the project's Python,

24
00:01:33,405 --> 00:01:37,855
and my goal is: make the tests pass.

25
00:01:37,955 --> 00:01:41,455
Two problems stop the tests from even starting.

26
00:01:42,105 --> 00:01:49,526
Step one: collections.Mapping was removed in Python 3.10. FixFirst shows the error,

27
00:01:49,566 --> 00:01:55,459
the rule behind its conclusion, the official Python documentation,

28
00:01:55,499 --> 00:01:58,905
and how the fix will be confirmed.

29
00:01:59,205 --> 00:02:04,618
Step two: helper is not a missing package. The project has a file called helpers.py,

30
00:02:04,658 --> 00:02:06,955
so the import is simply wrong.

31
00:02:07,655 --> 00:02:11,425
I make the two changes myself.

32
00:02:23,367 --> 00:02:30,171
And I press Check again. Both imports are confirmed. Now the tests actually run,

33
00:02:30,211 --> 00:02:34,667
and two failures appear that were hidden before.

34
00:02:34,767 --> 00:02:40,875
FixFirst updates the plan: numpy.float no longer exists,

35
00:02:40,915 --> 00:02:45,767
and one test needs a configuration value.

36
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:48,677
I fix both,

37
00:03:04,584 --> 00:03:06,534
and check again. All tests pass.

38
00:03:06,584 --> 00:03:12,638
All four problems are listed as fixed and verified, because a real test run passed,

39
00:03:12,678 --> 00:03:15,044
not because someone said so.

40
00:03:15,084 --> 00:03:19,934
And I can share a report with my teammate.

41
00:03:20,363 --> 00:03:23,213
Coding agents can use the same diagnosis.

42
00:03:23,563 --> 00:03:28,193
Through the Model Context Protocol, an agent asks FixFirst what is wrong,

43
00:03:28,233 --> 00:03:31,712
gets the next step with its evidence, makes the change,

44
00:03:31,752 --> 00:03:34,213
and asks FixFirst to check again.

45
00:03:34,563 --> 00:03:37,335
In our experiment on 18 repair tasks

46
00:03:37,375 --> 00:03:43,193
that were kept secret until the product was finished, a local model, Gemma,

47
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:49,363
repaired 39 percent of the tasks on its own, and 90 percent with FixFirst.

48
00:03:50,977 --> 00:03:57,677
We also tried FixFirst on 28 real bugs from open-source projects that it had never seen.

49
00:03:58,027 --> 00:04:04,811
When told which test fails, it closed 27 of them only after the real fix was in,

50
00:04:04,851 --> 00:04:08,527
and never called a bug fixed too early.

51
00:04:08,877 --> 00:04:12,576
In a small study with six developers,

52
00:04:12,616 --> 00:04:18,436
the median repair time was 23 percent lower with FixFirst,

53
00:04:18,476 --> 00:04:23,777
and they rated its usability about 80 out of 100.

54
00:04:24,127 --> 00:04:29,377
With six people, that is a trend, not a proof.

55
00:04:29,727 --> 00:04:31,437
FixFirst.

56
00:04:31,477 --> 00:04:34,427
Free, local, no account needed.

57
00:04:34,727 --> 00:04:41,877
Find what to fix first, and know when it is fixed.

